WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:03.977
- He's an Indian businessman,
investor, and philanthropist.

00:00:03.977 --> 00:00:05.740
He was the chairman of the Tata Group,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.926
a Mumbai-based company, from 1991 to 2012.

00:00:09.926 --> 00:00:11.980
The current revenues of the Tata Companies

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.590
taken together are over
$100 billion annually.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:19.757
He's Ratan Tata, and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.990
- Far more is gained by
walking on the shop floor

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.480
and talking to the people
and trying to communicate.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.553
One of my weaknesses has been, I think,

00:00:31.553 --> 00:00:33.860
I have never communicated
enough or adequately

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.875
with our people.

00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:38.625
Partly because I am a shy person,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:44.010
and, secondly, because I didn't realize

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:47.019
the importance of communication.

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:50.379
But I think communication
with the employees is

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:55.029
an exceedingly important function
that any company chairman

00:00:55.029 --> 00:00:58.766
has to do and be visible in that sense.

00:00:58.766 --> 00:01:02.299
The great successes, the
large companies of this world,

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:06.466
and there are many, and
many successful companies.

00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:11.022
Or are they the companies
that took chances,

00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:14.984
where individual started in garages,

00:01:15.861 --> 00:01:17.694
where ideas were born.

00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:24.747
Where did Microsoft, where
did Apple, where did Amazon,

00:01:24.747 --> 00:01:28.664
where did Google, where
did Facebook come from?

00:01:30.016 --> 00:01:33.188
They came from ideas that
people felt something

00:01:33.188 --> 00:01:38.009
could be done, and that they
could make a difference.

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:40.689
I probably would not
have returned to India,

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:43.731
except my grandmother fell ill.

00:01:43.731 --> 00:01:47.158
So I came back because she was ill.

00:01:47.158 --> 00:01:48.649
Had I stayed in the United States,

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:53.126
my life probably would
have been very different.

00:01:53.126 --> 00:01:57.159
I was very happy there
and very comfortable.

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:01.326
I had a very satisfying job
and when I came back here,

00:02:06.685 --> 00:02:10.852
I mentioned the first eight
years were frustration.

00:02:12.524 --> 00:02:15.205
My masters thought that the best way

00:02:15.205 --> 00:02:17.455
to teach me how to operate,

00:02:20.393 --> 00:02:23.765
when I don't think anyone at that time

00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:27.932
had thought that I would be
Mr. J.R.D. Tata's successor.

00:02:28.927 --> 00:02:32.340
So, I was one the shop
floor, shoveling limestone,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.281
working the furnaces,

00:02:34.281 --> 00:02:38.281
and I kept asking myself,
"Why am I doing this?"

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.607
And so, perhaps, looking back,
the most significant incident

00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:47.098
in my life was just my obstinacy

00:02:47.098 --> 00:02:49.655
to remain here, rather than to go back.

00:02:49.655 --> 00:02:53.153
My view is that trust is
an underlying foundation

00:02:53.153 --> 00:02:55.736
for the way people look at you,

00:02:58.908 --> 00:03:00.172
people look at your business,

00:03:00.172 --> 00:03:03.879
people look at the way
you operate your business.

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:07.503
Trust is the basis on which you live

00:03:07.503 --> 00:03:10.675
by your contractual obligations,

00:03:10.675 --> 00:03:15.234
and I think trust is more
important than anything else.

00:03:15.234 --> 00:03:19.401
Your psychological bond with
your customer and your worker

00:03:20.689 --> 00:03:23.155
and your stake holders in general.

00:03:23.155 --> 00:03:27.072
I think without that,
you run the risk of being

00:03:28.628 --> 00:03:32.795
a superficial business
entrepreneur, based on criteria,

00:03:35.203 --> 00:03:39.024
which are not, in fact, truly fundamental

00:03:39.024 --> 00:03:41.452
to the manner in which you do business.

00:03:41.452 --> 00:03:43.869
- Because, globally, Indians,

00:03:45.031 --> 00:03:48.614
whether they are
government or businessmen,

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:51.243
don't quite have the reputation

00:03:51.243 --> 00:03:55.076
of being the most ethical,
fairly or unfairly.

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:02.764
- I don't know whether one can generalize.

00:04:02.764 --> 00:04:06.050
But I would just say
that if you don't have

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:10.217
trust in your partner, in your
worker, in your management,

00:04:11.237 --> 00:04:15.434
and vice versa, if there's
no trust in the person

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:19.106
at the top, either, for
what he or she stands for

00:04:19.106 --> 00:04:21.606
or what their value system is,

00:04:25.074 --> 00:04:26.805
then you have a business

00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:31.579
that supports that level
of trust or lack of trust

00:04:31.579 --> 00:04:35.281
and that goes all the way to the consumer.

00:04:35.281 --> 00:04:37.749
He doesn't trust you for
what you provide him,

00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:41.034
he doesn't believe in
you claims and you have,

00:04:41.034 --> 00:04:43.951
in fact, a poorer form of business.

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:49.175
And, yet, ethics and values
becomes a very major part of it.

00:04:49.175 --> 00:04:52.183
If you think you cannot make a difference,

00:04:52.183 --> 00:04:56.350
I say you very well can make a
difference, if you so desire.

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:02.560
That you are humble as you look forward.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.310
If you speak or sit next
to a Nobel laureate,

00:05:07.575 --> 00:05:11.572
he never tells you that
he won a Nobel prize.

00:05:11.572 --> 00:05:12.846
Other people tell you.

00:05:12.846 --> 00:05:15.346
So, let humility be your best.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.589
I do know one hero I've always had that

00:05:20.589 --> 00:05:24.089
who no longer lives has been John Kennedy.

00:05:26.148 --> 00:05:26.981
- Oh, yes.

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:32.321
- Of course, I was very
much in the US at that time.

00:05:32.321 --> 00:05:37.165
That person stands out
still as very much my hero

00:05:37.165 --> 00:05:41.192
at that time and continues to
get me emotionally nostalgic

00:05:41.192 --> 00:05:43.757
about the days that I was there.

00:05:43.757 --> 00:05:45.115
- [Interviewer] That's true.

00:05:45.115 --> 00:05:46.438
- I mentioned when you took over

00:05:46.438 --> 00:05:49.484
that many external observers there

00:05:49.484 --> 00:05:50.843
thought you had large shoes to fill

00:05:50.843 --> 00:05:52.942
in taking over from your uncle.

00:05:52.942 --> 00:05:56.489
Now, with another transition
of power, Cyrus Pallonji Mistry

00:05:56.489 --> 00:06:00.311
has his own large shoes
to fill, that of yourself.

00:06:00.311 --> 00:06:02.123
I wonder what advice you would give Cyrus

00:06:02.123 --> 00:06:05.206
as he takes on this task as chairman.

00:06:07.808 --> 00:06:11.590
- I've told Cyrus that he
needs to be his own person,

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.590
that finally it was something I did.

00:06:15.455 --> 00:06:18.501
I realized the shoes that
I had to fill were far

00:06:18.501 --> 00:06:23.356
too big for me to mimic
the person I was following,

00:06:23.356 --> 00:06:25.939
and I just decided to be myself

00:06:27.671 --> 00:06:31.823
and do what I thought was
right would be the way to go.

00:06:31.823 --> 00:06:33.308
I've told Cyrus the same thing.

00:06:33.308 --> 00:06:37.475
I think he should feel that
he is being his own person

00:06:40.351 --> 00:06:43.934
and let his personality
and his conscience,

00:06:45.936 --> 00:06:47.539
if you might, drive him.

00:06:47.539 --> 00:06:52.230
And you shouldn't really
consider yourself successful

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:56.627
based on the prosperity
you're getting for yourself.

00:06:56.627 --> 00:07:00.452
But you should go home at
night feeling satisfied,

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:02.387
if you have made a difference.

00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:06.617
That difference is something
each one of use can make,

00:07:06.617 --> 00:07:10.784
that we'll have failures
and we've had frustrations,

00:07:13.145 --> 00:07:15.080
but it's a continued commitment

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:17.756
that we have to the world around us.

00:07:17.756 --> 00:07:21.996
My own personal feeling
is that it is not a trick,

00:07:21.996 --> 00:07:26.079
provided it is looked as
a driver for competition

00:07:29.556 --> 00:07:31.143
and a driver for innovation

00:07:31.143 --> 00:07:34.183
on the part of an Indian company.

00:07:34.183 --> 00:07:38.183
I'll take the automobile
industry as an example.

00:07:40.636 --> 00:07:43.536
Despite the fact that we produce

00:07:43.536 --> 00:07:46.315
about two million cars a year,

00:07:46.315 --> 00:07:50.910
there are only two
manufacturers of Indian cars.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.173
It's Mahindra and Tatas.

00:07:53.173 --> 00:07:55.759
Every other international manufacturer

00:07:55.759 --> 00:07:58.342
is here in one form or another.

00:08:01.349 --> 00:08:04.719
Yes, there are disadvantages
because scale, et cetera,

00:08:04.719 --> 00:08:07.927
is far bigger in the
foreign manufacturers.

00:08:07.927 --> 00:08:12.488
But if you look before that,
the fifty years of automobiles,

00:08:12.488 --> 00:08:15.759
we had two manufacturers
who never increased

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:19.654
their production for
more than 50,000 cars.

00:08:19.654 --> 00:08:22.063
Who never changed their model,

00:08:22.063 --> 00:08:25.896
who never innovated, who
never gave the market

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:29.416
anything other than what they produced,

00:08:29.416 --> 00:08:31.147
and the market just bought what they,

00:08:31.147 --> 00:08:35.961
you waited seven years for
a car, 10 years for a car.

00:08:35.961 --> 00:08:38.961
So, if you look at the two examples,

00:08:40.959 --> 00:08:42.626
has India benefited?

00:08:44.143 --> 00:08:45.476
I would say yes.

00:08:47.036 --> 00:08:50.690
Do the two Indian manufacturers gain

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:52.870
anything from this open market?

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:57.269
Yes, because we are driven
to compete with those people.

00:08:57.269 --> 00:09:01.337
And the day we look at that
they're meant to drive us out,

00:09:01.337 --> 00:09:04.253
I think we, as a nation, would lose out.

00:09:04.253 --> 00:09:07.708
All of you are being trained be innovative

00:09:07.708 --> 00:09:11.875
and hopefully out-do their
(mumbles) when they come here.

00:09:14.241 --> 00:09:18.776
It's our job as employers
to encourage you to do that,

00:09:18.776 --> 00:09:20.753
to encourage you to break new ground,

00:09:20.753 --> 00:09:22.753
to think out of the box.

00:09:26.149 --> 00:09:30.316
And it's for you to do it
and for us to support it.

00:09:31.872 --> 00:09:33.928
So, I don't think we should look

00:09:33.928 --> 00:09:36.261
at them and see as a threat.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:39.024
I think we should look at them

00:09:39.024 --> 00:09:43.191
as a motivator to be more
competitive and beat them.

00:09:44.071 --> 00:09:48.120
The greatest pleasure I've
had is trying to do something

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.287
that everybody says could not be done.

00:09:54.027 --> 00:09:56.110
I'll just share, perhaps,

00:09:58.761 --> 00:10:02.511
a moment in time which
I will always cherish.

00:10:04.452 --> 00:10:08.369
I decided that India
could produce its own car.

00:10:09.373 --> 00:10:13.540
Everybody, my friends overseas
in the automobile business,

00:10:16.686 --> 00:10:18.117
said that this couldn't be done,

00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:20.819
we had to go through a collaboration

00:10:20.819 --> 00:10:23.486
to get know-how, get technology.

00:10:25.781 --> 00:10:29.685
But we undertook to produce this car.

00:10:29.685 --> 00:10:32.518
You saw images of it in the video,

00:10:33.871 --> 00:10:36.085
it was called the Indica.

00:10:36.085 --> 00:10:40.252
And we produced it in India,
totally off Indian content.

00:10:43.536 --> 00:10:47.203
As we got close to
putting it in the market,

00:10:50.825 --> 00:10:54.992
my friends in India somewhat
distanced themselves from me.

00:10:56.374 --> 00:10:57.960
Otherwise known as distancing

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.806
yourself from failure.
(audience laughs)

00:11:01.806 --> 00:11:03.973
And when the car came out,

00:11:07.002 --> 00:11:11.626
I suddenly felt that I didn't
have a friend in the world,

00:11:11.626 --> 00:11:13.964
and that all the warnings that people

00:11:13.964 --> 00:11:17.797
had given me were probably
going to come true.

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:21.364
But the car did come out.

00:11:21.364 --> 00:11:24.557
It did earn a 20% market share

00:11:24.557 --> 00:11:27.601
and we showed that we could do something.

00:11:27.601 --> 00:11:31.768
Throughout the time I've
been involved in business,

00:11:33.586 --> 00:11:38.172
there hasn't been anything that
has equaled the excitement,

00:11:38.172 --> 00:11:41.543
the reward, and the exhilaration

00:11:41.543 --> 00:11:45.648
that the car industry has
provided, and can provide.

00:11:45.648 --> 00:11:46.718
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:46.718 --> 00:11:49.969
I made this video
because HiLo asked me to,

00:11:49.969 --> 00:11:51.288
so if there is a famous entrepreneur

00:11:51.288 --> 00:11:53.580
that you want me to profile next,

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:56.049
leave it in the comments below,
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:56.049 --> 00:11:58.595
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:11:58.595 --> 00:12:00.532
had the biggest impact on you.

00:12:00.532 --> 00:12:02.869
Leave it in the comments, I
will join in the discussion.

00:12:02.869 --> 00:12:05.327
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:12:05.327 --> 00:12:07.160
and I'll see you soon.

